course: dictionary
lesson: 7637
title: Productivity: getting more done
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Productivity means how much a person, machine or place makes or does in a certain time. «Productivity rose when the new machines came.» That means more work or goods got finished in the same time.
explain: Think of productivity as work done per hour. If two bakers each make 50 loaves in an hour, they have the same productivity. If one makes 80 loaves, that baker has higher productivity. «High productivity» means a lot gets done, and «boost productivity» means to make it grow.
explain: Productivity is about using time well. It is not the same as working harder for longer, because the question is always how much is made in the time you have. Ask yourself: how much came out, and how long did it take?
fact: Productivity means how much work, goods or results are produced in a certain time.
fact: Productivity is often measured as output per worker or per hour.
fact: «Productivity per hour» compares how much is made with the time it took to make it.
fact: In science, productivity can mean how much plant or animal life a place produces, such as a field or a lake.
fact: Investment is spending now, such as buying new machines, to get more later. Investment can help productivity grow.
exercise: «Productivity rose when the new machines came.» What does productivity mean here? | The number of workers who went home early | A kind of weather | *How much work or goods were produced in a certain time | The colour of the machines
exercise: Ella's class made 40 paper boats in one hour. Sam's class made 60 in one hour. Whose productivity was higher? | *Sam's, because more boats were made in the same time | Ella's, because her class started first | They were exactly the same, so neither was higher
exercise: In a geography lesson, a farm's productivity is how much food it grows. Which is the best example of this? | A farm that is far from the town | A farm with the most animals that have names | A farm with the biggest gate | *A farm that grows a lot of wheat each summer
test: A factory wants to boost productivity. What is it trying to do? | *Make more in the same amount of time | Make less and rest more | Close early on Fridays | Stop using all its machines
test: In science, productivity can describe a lake. What does this mean? | How deep the lake is | How many boats are on the lake | How loud the lake is | *How much plant and animal life the lake produces
exam: «Productivity is how much is made per worker; investment is spending now to get more later.» Which choice is an example of investment? | Waiting for the workers to go home | *Buying new machines now so that more can be made later | Selling all the machines today | Eating lunch at the factory
